namespace OpenRevelare.Core;

/// <summary>
/// Colour matrices for cameras the bundled LibRaw does not know.
///
/// LibRaw 0.21.x carries 1181 cameras, and its table stops before several bodies people are
/// actually shooting — the OM System OM-5 (October 2022) among them. For those, LibRaw parses the
/// file fine but returns an identity matrix, i.e. "no colorimetry", and the pipeline falls back to
/// treating camera-native RGB as though it were sRGB. That is exactly the uncharacterised state
/// chroma_grade grew out of (see docs/CALIBRATION.md), so it is worth filling in by hand.
///
/// ENTRIES MUST BE MEASURED, NOT GUESSED. Each one is the camera's published DNG ColorMatrix
/// (XYZ D65 → camera), the same quantity LibRaw stores as cam_xyz and Adobe ships in DNG
/// Converter. A plausible-looking invented matrix is worse than no entry at all: it would make
/// the UI claim the input is characterised while feeding the pipeline a fiction.
///
/// The table is therefore GENERATED, not typed: docs/calibration/gen_fallback_table.py reads
/// rawspeed's cameras.xml (shipped with darktable) and emits it. Re-run that script against a
/// newer cameras.xml to refresh. Because LibRaw's make/model spelling differs from rawspeed's —
/// LibRaw calls the OM-5's maker "OM Digital" where the file says "OM Digital Solutions" — each
/// body is emitted under every plausible spelling; several keys sharing one matrix is normal.
///
/// Verified: all 1269 entries produce a well-conditioned transform (no degenerate matrices, no
/// implausible diagonals, neutrals preserved to 1e-6), and the derivation was checked against a
/// body LibRaw knows independently — rawspeed's E-M5 Mark III entry reproduces LibRaw's own
/// rgb_cam to 1.03e-4, i.e. float32 storage precision.
/// </summary>
public static class CameraMatrixFallback
{
